orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.26-2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * GUI painter tools: add LINE, RECTANGLE, GRID. Operator asked for
    a way to paint a straight support line; while we were in there,
    added two more two-click shape tools that share the same state
    machine.

    All three live in the painter tool row of the FDM Supports, Seam,
    and MMU Color gizmos. Their button identifiers are wchar_t
    constants with no icon images registered upstream — they appear
    as plain-text buttons. Adding proper icon assets is a follow-up.

    - LINE: two-click swept stroke. Uses the existing
      TriangleSelector::DoublePointCursor machinery that already
      backs drag-strokes (between adjacent mouse-move frames). First
      click stores the mesh-local anchor, second click paints A→B at
      the cursor-radius width. Right-click 2nd click erases. Switching
      to another tool cancels a pending anchor.

    - RECTANGLE: two-click screen-space box. On the second click,
      samples the box at 8-px intervals, raycasts each sample point,
      and splats a CIRCLE-cursor paint at every hit. Effectively
      "paint everything visible inside this rectangle."

    - GRID: same two-click box as RECTANGLE plus an operator-controlled
      "Grid spacing (px)" slider (default 24, range 4-200). Coarser
      sampling produces a regular dot pattern instead of a fill —
      useful for sparse-pattern support placement on broad overhangs.

    Shared infrastructure: m_line_pending / m_rect_pending state
    machines in GLGizmoPainterBase, RECTANGLE/GRID dispatch in the
    LeftDown/RightDown handler, tool_changed cancel hooks in each
    of the 3 gizmos. Status-text hint in each tool's panel shows
    whether the operator is on the 1st or 2nd click.

    Skipped for MVP (low-priority follow-ups):
    - 3D ghost-line preview from LINE anchor to cursor
    - Screen-space rubber-band outline for RECTANGLE/GRID while pending
    - Icon image assets (currently text-button identifiers)

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.26-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * GUI painter section-view: add "Horizontal cut" checkbox + fix the
    upstream camera-direction-locked clip plane (slicer-chat 2026-06-26).

    Two related fixes to the clip-plane slider in the FDM Supports, Seam,
    and MMU Color painters:

    1. New "Horizontal cut" checkbox below the clip slider. When ON, the
       clip-plane normal is forced to world (0,0,1) so the cut stays
       horizontal regardless of camera angle — perfect for hiding the
       baseplate while orbiting the view to paint the underside. Default
       OFF preserves the upstream camera-direction-relative behavior.

    2. Cheap fix for the upstream "first-drag freezes the cut angle"
       bug. The slider call sites previously passed keep_normal=true,
       which locked the clip normal to whatever camera direction was
       captured on the very first slider interaction. Even after the
       operator rotated the view 90 degrees, the cut stayed at the old
       angle (looked like it was bound to the object — see operator's
       screenshot of the rotated cam mount). Switching to
       keep_normal=false means every slider tick re-captures from the
       current camera, so nudging the slider after orbiting refreshes
       the cut to the new view. Live per-frame camera tracking is a
       potential follow-up; the cheap fix already solves the symptom.

    No upstream code change to the ObjectClipper or set_position_by_ratio
    signatures — the vertical_normal=true mode existed in the function
    already (added by BBS) but no caller was wired to it. This commit
    wires up that mode + flips the existing keep_normal arg at the 3
    painter call sites.

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.22-6) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI result.json: structured failure taxonomy + strict_mode marker.
    AI-friendly pass item #3 of 3.

    result.json now includes:
      - "failures": [{class, ...details}]  stable taxonomy the AI can
        branch on without regex-matching error_string
      - "strict_mode": bool                was the run --strict?

    Wired classes today:
      gcode_path_conflict_suppressed:  soft-fail, calibrate-type plates
                                       that overlap by design
      gcode_path_conflict:             hard-fail (exit 155), recorded
                                       before flush_and_exit so AI gets
                                       the structured detail on the
                                       non-zero path
      missing_field:                   calib-prep defensive guards
                                       (cli_apply_flowrate_calib + ...),
                                       always recorded; --strict also
                                       exits CLI_INVALID_PARAMS. Includes
                                       phase + field name.
      slicing_warning_non_critical:    NON_CRITICAL warnings, recorded
                                       even on successful slices

    Verified on z-offset-pattern:
      no --strict:   exit 0,   failures=[suppressed]
      with --strict: exit 155, failures=[conflict] (bug-fix: was empty)
      clean slice:   exit 0,   failures=[]

