orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.22-3) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI AI-friendly pass: --inspect-mesh action + --strict mode.
    Slicer-chat 2026-06-22 — followup to the orient-feedback round.

    --inspect-mesh (new action, parallel to --info):
      Emits a structured JSON summary of the loaded model(s) to stdout
      and exits 0 without slicing. Per-object: name, triangle_count,
      instance_count, bbox_mesh_local (min/max/size/centroid — the
      frame --ground-face-point uses), bbox_world + instance_offset,
      and the top-N largest planar-face clusters sorted by area with
      ready-to-use --ground-face-normal commands.

      Runs AFTER transforms, so doubles as a dry-run: pass any
      combination of --rotate-* / --ground-* / --scale / --center-on-bed
      to preview the post-transform geometry without slicing. The AI
      can iterate orient strategy at ~1s/call instead of ~30s/call.

      Verified on Camera_Stand_Base.stl: top cluster (0,0,-1) 2253 mm²;
      with --rotate-y 90 → bbox_world.z 20→27 (rotation visible); with
      --ground-face-normal 1,0,0 → bbox_world.z 27→54 (orient visible).

    --strict (new flag in actions def):
      Re-elevates three classes of silent-pass conditions to non-zero
      exits, for CI / scripted / AI pipelines that should never ship
      a subtly broken slice:
        (1) --calibrate-type gcode-path conflicts (today demoted to a
            warning because calibration plates intentionally overlap)
        (2) NON_CRITICAL slicing warnings (today only logged)
        (3) CLI calibration prep that fell back to a defensive default
            (cli_apply_flowrate_calib's missing_field_error paths,
            future cli_apply_* generators that follow the same pattern)

      Sanity: --strict --calibrate-type z-offset-pattern now exits
      155 (CLI_GCODE_PATH_CONFLICTS) instead of slicing silently;
      --strict on a normal slice + on calibrate-types without overlap
      regression-tested to exit 0.

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.22-2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI orient: auto-lift to Z=0 after grounding (slicer-chat 2026-06-22
    cammount-orient-feedback). The new --ground-* / --lay-flat primitives
    landed but had a hidden footgun: after Quaterniond rotation the
    grounded face often sat at z=-1e-9 from FP rounding, and the slicer
    rejected the model with the cryptic "No layers were detected" error.
    Operator had to know to add --ensure-on-bed manually.

    Root cause: my apply_ground_rotation called ModelObject::ensure_on_bed,
    but that's a no-op for CLI-loaded instances — the lift skips any
    instance whose auto_drop flag is false, and CLI loaders default it
    to false. (Auto_drop is the GUI's "moved-by-mouse → drop-to-bed" flag.)

    Fix: apply the lift directly in apply_ground_rotation. For each
    instance, compute its world-coord bbox min.z and shift the Z offset
    by -min.z. Works whether the rotation landed the part slightly below
    OR slightly above Z=0 — both cases now snap to exactly Z=0.

    Sanity verified on Camera_Stand_Base.stl + Klipper Cube Test:
      --ground-face-normal 1,0,0   270 layers from Z=0.2 (was: error)
      --ground-largest-face 1      100 layers from Z=0.2 (no-op rotate)
      --ground-largest-face 1 --center-on-bed 1 also clean

    Other feedback items from the brief NOT addressed in this version
    (separate work, lower priority):
    - "; estimated first layer printing time = 1s" — appears to be a
      time-estimator bug in PrintObject's per-layer time calc, not in
      the orient code. Investigate separately.
    - "setup params error" doesn't say which flag is wrong when a
      coInt flag is given as a naked switch — comes from the upstream
      m_config.validate() path, broader CLI parser change.

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.22-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI: ground-a-face primitives + flow-yolo / pa-tower / rotate segfault
    fixes. Today's pass closes the operator-feedback briefs:

    NEW orient primitives (slicer-chat 2026-06-22 cammount-orient brief):
      --ground-largest-face / --lay-flat  Find the largest planar face
        cluster (sum of coplanar triangle areas by quantized normal) and
        rotate so it sits on Z=0. Covers ~80% of "this part has one
        obvious orientation" cases.
      --ground-face-normal NX,NY,NZ       Rotate so the face whose
        mesh-local normal best matches the given vector sits on Z=0.
      --ground-face-point X,Y,Z           Find the triangle containing
        the given mesh-local point (post-mesh-centering coords) and
        ground its face. Disambiguates overlapping inner+outer faces
        by preferring the normal pointing outward from the bbox centroid.
      --center-on-bed 1                   Translate so XY bbox centroid
        lands at the bed center. Derived from printable_area or falls
        back to (125,110) for a Core One footprint.
    All implemented in src/slic3r/Utils/MeshOrient.{cpp,hpp}, mirroring
    the Selection::flattening_rotate math (Quaterniond setFromTwoVectors).
    CLI argument order is preserved by opt_order so flags compose naturally.

    flow-yolo SEGFAULT (slicer-chat 2026-06-22 flow-yolo-segfault brief):
      cli_apply_flowrate_calib derefed nullptr config options when a
      preset's `inherits` chain didn't resolve (typical without
      --datadir). Now every option lookup is guarded; missing keys log
      a clear error pointing at the field + suggest --datadir, function
      returns cleanly instead of crashing. Side effect: flow-* variants
      now slice successfully without --datadir too (user preset values
      alone are sufficient).

    pa-tower SIGSEGV (uncommitted carryover from earlier today):
      find_optimal_PA_speed read outer_wall_speed/nozzle_diameter/
      filament_max_volumetric_speed without null checks. CLI path passes
      a config that hasn't yet been filled by Print::apply(), so optional
      keys are absent and assert(...) was a no-op under NDEBUG. Local
      scalar/vector_at lambdas fall back to each option's ConfigDef
      default. GUI behavior unchanged. Two-arg get_abs_value() now throws
      ConfigurationError on null option instead of segfaulting (matches
      the single-arg overload behavior).

    Pre-existing CLI rotate crash (caught while testing the orient code):
      PartPlate::check_outside derefed m_plater (null in CLI mode) when
      an instance dipped below SINKING_Z_THRESHOLD. Triggered the same
      way by --rotate-y 90 / --ground-face-normal 1,0,0 / any large
      rotation. Added m_plater null guard parallel to the existing
      pattern at lines 196/672.

    Sanity matrix on Camera_Stand_Base.stl + Klipper Cube Test:
      --ground-largest-face: bottom face cluster 2253 mm², lay-flat ok
      --lay-flat: alias verified identical to above
      --ground-face-normal 0,0,-1: best_dot=1.0, no-op rotation
      --ground-face-normal 1,0,0: 90° around Y (was prior crash via
        the null-Plater path; now works after the guard)
      --ground-face-normal 0,1,0: 90° around X
      --ground-face-point 0,0,-10: bottom face, 225 mm², outward chosen
        correctly over interior cross-section
      --ground-face-point 9999,9999,9999: exit 254 with clear error
      --center-on-bed 1: shift computed and applied
      flow-yolo-* without --datadir: now slices successfully
      --rotate-y 90 (regression): works (was pre-existing CLI crash)

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.21-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI z-ladder: visual aids default OFF + working toggle plumbing.
    Slicer-chat 2026-06-21 — operators using a flatbed scanner identify
    bands by Y position, so the AI-vision fiducials and DPI scale bar are
    unused waste on every ladder run (~2 minutes + ~2g across 4 runs).
    Defaults all flipped to OFF (parallel to --zladder-id-dots flip on -19).
    Operators who DO use vision/DPI explicitly opt in.

    Toggle plumbing was also broken — bool flags accepted any trailing
    value silently but applied none of it:
      --no-zladder-fiducials       Invalid (BBS-disabled, won't fix)
      --zladder-fiducials=false    silently ignored (deserialize fails,
                                    caller didn't check)
      --zladder-fiducials 0        silently ignored (bool parser doesn't
                                    consume next arg, Config.cpp:1660)
      --zladder-fiducials false    same
    Net: bool flags were ON-only — once a default landed, operator was
    stuck. Fix: change the 3 flag types from coBool to coInt (0|1).
    Matches the existing --arrange convention. Both `--flag 0|1` and
    `--flag=0|1` now work; invalid values are loud errors instead of
    silent ignores.

    Sanity matrix verified — all 8 forms behave correctly:
      (no flag)                    → off (fid=0)
      --zladder-fiducials 1        → on  (fid=40)
      --zladder-fiducials 0        → off (was silent ignore)
      --zladder-fiducials=1        → on
      --zladder-fiducials=0        → off
      --zladder-fiducials=false    → loud error (was silent ignore)
      --zladder-fiducials false    → loud error (was silent ignore)
      --no-zladder-fiducials       → Invalid option (intentional)

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.19-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI z-ladder: flip --zladder-id-dots default to OFF. After 4+ iterations
    on ASA + PLA, the per-band ID dots (1+2+3+4+5 = 15 small 0.8mm boxes east
    of the pad) proved useless in practice — the pad never fragments (single
    concentric-fill 60x75mm rectangle), and the fiducials + scale bar already
    identify bands by Y position. Default OFF saves ~3s + ~0.6mm filament per
    print and removes east-of-pad clutter from every scan. Pass the explicit
    --zladder-id-dots flag to restore dots if a print does fragment.
    Sidecar JSON also omits `id_dots:N` per band when dots aren't emitted,
    so the JSON matches the physical plate geometry.

  * calib: default-init Calib_Params scalar fields (start/end/step,
    print_numbers, freqStart{X,Y}/freqEnd{X,Y}, test_model). Pre-existing
    upstream — the user-provided default ctor only initialized `mode`, so
    `Calib_Params{}` value-init left several scalars uninitialized.
    cli_zladder_get_calib_params doing `out = Calib_Params{}` made GCC start
    warning. Neutral defaults (0/false). No behavior change.

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.18-4) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI flow-rate: mirror per-object YOLO overrides globally so the gcode
    header reports what was actually emitted, and make --flow-brim
    actually toggle brim_type globally. Slicer-chat reported that a
    --calibrate-type flow-yolo-recommended slice had a gcode header
    showing wall_loops=2, sparse_infill_pattern=gyroid,
    top_surface_pattern=monotonicline, brim_type=no_brim even though
    --flow-brim was passed. The per-object overrides in
    cli_apply_flowrate_calib were correct, but the gcode header at the
    top of the file reflects the GLOBAL print_config, not per-object
    overrides — so the header lied about what the slicer used, breaking
    downstream verification + the brim flag entirely.

    Fix mirrors the per-object setters globally too:
      wall_loops=1, top_shell_layers=5, bottom_shell_layers=2,
      sparse_infill_density=35%, skirt_loops=0,
      sparse_infill_pattern=rectilinear,
      top_surface_pattern=params.pattern (archimedean by default),
    plus brim_type/brim_width/brim_object_gap driven by params.brim_enabled.

    Note on the values: the original brief asked for
    100% / 1+1 shells / archimedean-sparse per the OrcaSlicer wiki's
    "Archimedean Chords / YOLO Recommended" page. Cross-checked against
    upstream Plater::adjust_settings_for_flowrate_calib — the upstream
    GUI uses 35% / 2+5 shells / rectilinear-sparse, and has done so since
    PR #6479 "New YOLO flow rate calibration" (Aug 2024). The wiki page
    is pre-PR-#6479 and stale. Values here mirror the active GUI,
    intentionally identical to the per-object block 50 lines above. The
    archimedean *signal* still appears on the visible top surface via
    top_surface_pattern, which is what YOLO actually reads.

    Sanity verified on Klipper Cube Test + ANYCUBIC PLA:
      T1 (defaults):     header shows 1/35%/2/5/rectilinear/archimedean
      T2 (--flow-brim --flow-brim-width 3): brim_type=outer_only width=3
      T3 (--flow-height 4.0): 20 layer changes + block_height_mm=4 (regression OK)
      T4 PRINT_START preserved across all three.

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.18-3) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI flow-rate: --calibrate-type flow-yolo-coarse and --flow-height mm.
    Slicer-chat ask after a PLA YOLO needed seeding at 1.05 just to shift
    the recommended ±0.05 range to catch a 1.04-1.06 optimum — if the
    optimum had been at 0.92 or 1.15, recommended would have missed.

    flow-yolo-coarse: 5 procedural blocks at modifiers ±0.10, ±0.05, 0
    (so 0.90..1.10 coverage at seed=1.0). Trades resolution for range.
    Same per-object pattern + brim handling as the existing flow-yolo
    variants; loaded into the standard cli_apply_flowrate_calib hook.
    Workflow: coarse first (one-time per filament+printer), then
    recommended for per-spool refinement.

    --flow-height mm (default 3.0, was hardcoded 2.0). Applies to all
    flow-* variants. Replaces the hardcoded "9" in
      zscale = (first_layer_h + 9 * layer_height) / 2
    with
      n_layers = round(flow_height_mm / layer_height)
      zscale   = (first_layer_h + (n_layers - 1) * layer_height) / 2
    Backward compat: --flow-height 2.0 reproduces the 10-layer output
    bit-for-bit (modulo the new sidecar field). Default 3.0 → 15 layers,
    more mass for peel + readable surface.

    JSON sidecar gains block_height_mm field (all flow-* variants).

    Verified on ASA + Klipper:
      coarse defaults:    15 layers, 22m 12s, 5 blocks ±0.10/±0.05/0
      recommended default: 15 layers, 40m 49s, 11 blocks (was 35m at 2mm)
      recommended -h 2.0: 10 layers, 32m 44s (backward compat)
      coarse -h 4.0:      20 layers, 25m 49s
    PRINT_START preserved verbatim.

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.18-2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI z-ladder: emit fiducials + scale_bar + per-band id_dots in JSON
    sidecar, plus physical id-dot clusters east of the pad. Slicer chat
    reported both missing. The cli_build_zladder generator was creating
    fiducial L-shapes and the scale bar comb as physical objects but the
    JSON emitter never serialized them, and id_dots was never wired at
    all. One coordinated patch:
      - JSON: fiducials array (4 entries with x/y in bed coords, id in
        TL/TR/BR/BL, notch_corner in NW/NE/SE/SW)
      - JSON: scale_bar block with origin/length_mm/tick_count
      - JSON: each band entry gains id_dots = index+1
      - Physical: per-band ID dot clusters added east of the pad
        (band 0 = 1 dot, ..., band 4 = 5 dots; 0.8×0.8mm each). 15 dots
        total for the default 5-band plate.
    AI-vision tooling can attribute a peeled fragment to its band by
    counting adjacent dots.
    Verified on ASA + Klipper: same 7 Z_ADJUST emissions as before
    (modulation unaffected), JSON now has fiducials/scale_bar/id_dots.

orcaslicer-dev (2.4.0~dev-2026.06.18-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * CLI: add --calibrate-type z-ladder-banded / z-ladder-ramp.
    Single-pad Z-offset sweep — replaces the interactive LCD-babystepping
    workflow with a baked-in Z modulation across the pad. Print, scan
    (or eyeball), pick the band that looks best, Z_OFFSET_SAVE.

    Banded variant: N discrete Z-offset bands (default 5) across the Y
    axis of the pad. Each band 15mm tall (default). Default range
    -0.10..+0.10mm in 0.05mm steps. Clean visual transitions between
    bands; AI vision attributes by Y position.

    Ramp variant: continuous Z gradient over the pad height (default
    75mm tall). Z updated per fill line via linear Y interpolation.
    For sub-mm fine-tuning after banded got you close.

    Implementation:
    - New CalibMode::Calib_ZLadder_Banded / Calib_ZLadder_Ramp enum values
    - Calib_Params extended with ladder_steps, ladder_band_height_mm,
      ladder_pad_y_lo, ladder_pad_y_hi (all in MODEL coords)
    - New GCode::_ladder_modulation_for_path helper called from both
      extrude_path() AND extrude_multi_path() — emits SET_GCODE_OFFSET
      Z_ADJUST=<delta> per fill line based on path.polyline.first_point().y()
    - End-of-layer-1 restore in the calib_mode switch at GCode.cpp:4604+
    - Pad uses ipMonotonicLine bottom-surface pattern so the slicer emits
      each fill line as a separate ExtrusionPath in monotonic Y order
      (rectilinear packs many lines into one multipath where first_point.y
      only reflects the start of the entire snake — banding gets garbled).
    - cli_build_zladder procedural generator: solid pad + 4 fiducials
      (L-shapes with unique-corner notches) + scale-bar comb.

    New CLI flags:
      --zladder-steps N (default 5)
      --zladder-start mm (default -0.10)
      --zladder-end mm (default +0.10)
      --zladder-band-height mm (default 15)
      --zladder-height mm (ramp only, default 75)
      --zladder-width mm (default 60)
      --zladder-fiducials (default ON)
      --zladder-scale-bar (default ON)

    JSON sidecar gains z-ladder-banded and z-ladder-ramp branches with
    params, pad_bbox, and (for banded) per-band z_offset_mm + y_low_mm
    + y_high_mm in BED coords.

    Verified on ASA + Klipper Core One:
      banded defaults:  1 layer, 2m 39s, 7 Z_ADJUST insertions
                        (5 band transitions + start no-op + end restore)
      ramp defaults:    1 layer, 2m 39s, 177 Z_ADJUST insertions
                        (one per fill line for continuous gradient)
    PRINT_START contract preserved verbatim across both variants.

