  * CLI z-offset-pattern v8 — compact layout + structural grid mesh.
    Reported from the slicer chat: the v7 plate was 100×100mm with mostly
    empty space, and the single thin struts wouldn't survive a peel
    attempt (visible as fragile single lines in the GUI preview). Three
    coordinated changes:

    1. COMPACT LAYOUT. Plate default reduced from 100mm to 60mm; zones
       reduced from 30mm to 18mm; spacing/positions adjusted to pack
       tightly. New plate footprint: 71×71mm (was 111×111mm — 60% area
       reduction). Constants in cli_build_zcal_pattern adapted:
         fid_off = half - 3.0 (was -5)
         corner_off = half - 9.0 (was -12)
         row_y = 0 (was +8, now centered)
         zone_spacing = 1.5 (was 3.0)
         scale_bar_y = -(zone/2 + 5) (now scales with zone size)
       Pass --zcal-size 100 --zcal-zone-size 30 for the v7 wide layout.

    2. STRUCTURAL GRID MESH. New 4mm-pitch rectangular grid of thin
       single-perimeter lines covering the frame interior. Each grid
       line is a 0.45mm-wide × 0.20mm-thick box, axis-aligned. Lines
       bond to every island they touch via 0.5mm overlap at the
       boundary (perimeter union → real physical bond). The grid acts
       like rebar in concrete: tension is distributed across many small
       connections so no single failure point can tear the peel.
       Default segment count for 60×60 plate: 87 grid segments.

    3. CONFIGURABLE GRID CLIPPING. By default the grid lines STOP at
       zone/fiducial/C-loop/scale-bar boundaries so the calibration
       signal area stays clean for scanner analysis (each line is
       broken into sub-segments that fit between objects, each
       sub-segment extends 0.5mm INTO the adjacent island at both
       endpoints for bond). Pass --zcal-grid-over-zones to let lines
       cross zones fully — fewer segments (54 instead of 87 for
       defaults) but the scanner sees grid lines inside the zone
       footprint and must mask them out via the metadata strut list.

    New CLI flags:
      --zcal-grid              default ON
      --zcal-grid-pitch mm     default 4.0
      --zcal-grid-over-zones   default OFF

    Verified on ASA + Klipper:
      defaults (60×60 + grid, stop at borders): 2 layers, 0.69g,
        4m 31s, 87 grid segments + 35 non-grid objects
      grid_over_zones (60×60 + grid crosses):    0.83g, 5m 12s, 54 segs
      no_grid (60×60 without grid):              0.55g, ~3m, 35 objects
      wide v7 layout (--zcal-size 100):          1.24g, 7m 55s, 130 segs

    PRINT_START contract preserved verbatim. Conflict detector
    suppression from v6.3 still in effect (the grid intentionally
    overlaps every island for bond).

  * CLI z-offset-pattern v7 — scale bar comb mesh. The scale bar was the
    last source of peel fragmentation: baseline + 11 ticks shipped as
    12 separate ModelObjects that scattered on peel. v7 replaces them
    with a SINGLE custom-vertex polygon mesh containing all 12 elements
    plus a strut to the frame. ~60-vertex CCW polygon: 4 baseline
    corners + 11 ticks × 4 vertices each + 4 strut vertices, with the
    strut detour positioned between ticks 5 and 6 (x∈[0.275, 0.725])
    so it clears all tick X ranges. Baseline widened from 10mm to 11mm
    so the end ticks (i=0, i=10) fit comfortably within the baseline
    bbox. Triangulation::triangulate handles the ~60-vertex concave
    polygon cleanly. The strut overlaps the frame bottom inner edge by
    0.5mm — slicer unions perimeters at slice time, peel is solid.
    Net object count: 46 → 35 (12 separate scale_* objects → 1 comb).
    JSON sidecar gains a 12th strut entry "scale_bar_strut".
    Verified on ASA + Klipper: 2 layers, 0.88g, 4m 12s.
  * CLI z-offset-pattern v6.3 — peel-as-one-piece via merged geometry +
    conflict-detector suppression. Reported from the slicer chat: even
    with the 2026.06.16-5 peelable frame, the print fragments on peel
    because fiducials / zones / C-loops sit as separate ModelObjects
    inside the frame ring with no mechanical bond. Three fixes land here:

    1. cli_emit_calib_outputs / cli_build_zcal_pattern now generate
       fid+strut as a single custom-vertex polygon mesh per fiducial.
       Each fiducial is an L-shape (5×5mm pad with a 1.5×1.5mm notch in
       a unique corner — NW/NE/SE/SW — so AI vision can identify any
       fragment even if peel scatters), with a 0.45mm thin tail extending
       from the side OPPOSITE the notch toward the frame. Polygon
       triangulated via libslic3r::Triangulation, extruded by 0.20mm,
       added as ONE ModelObject — no inter-object conflict between pad
       and strut. Same merge applied to zone_S+strut.
    2. Zone G, zone W, and the 4 corner C-loops now get standalone
       struts whose endpoints OVERLAP the island top edge AND the frame
       inner edge by 0.5mm each. With overlap = 0.5mm, the slicer's
       perimeter union welds the strut to both endpoints at slice time —
       producing physical bond on the printed part.
    3. CLI_GCODE_PATH_CONFLICTS check suppressed for any --calibrate-type
       slice in OrcaSlicer.cpp. The overlapping geometry is intentional
       for calibration plates; the conflict warning is downgraded to a
       log message so the slice succeeds. Non-calibration slices still
       enforce the check.

    JSON sidecar grows a "struts" array with all 11 entries
    (4 fid+tail, 1 zone_S+tail, 1 zone_G, 1 zone_W, 4 C-loop struts) so
    AI-vision tooling can mask out the strut regions during zone
    analysis. Fiducial entries grow notch_corner = "NW"|"NE"|"SE"|"SW".

    New CLI flags introduced in v6.3:
      --zcal-struts          (default ON)
      --zcal-fiducial-ids    (default ON, drives the per-corner notches)
      --zcal-zone-ids        (default ON, 1/2/3 small ID dots beside S/G/W)

    Verified end-to-end on ASA + Klipper:
      defaults:      2 layers, 0.87g, 4m 11s, 11 struts in JSON
      no-extras:     2 layers, v5-compat, 3m 57s
      no-frame:      1 layer,  v3-compat, 1m 28s
    PRINT_START contract preserved verbatim in all three.

    Known follow-up (v7): scale bar baseline + 11 ticks still fragment
    on peel (each is a separate ModelObject). Will need to be replaced
    with a single comb polygon mesh — too much code for v6.3.

  * CLI z-offset-pattern: add optional peelable structural frame (default
    ON) (originally landed 2026.06.16-5). Reported from the slicer chat: scanning a single-layer print on
    the textured PEI sheet doesn't work (sheet sits 2-3mm above the
    flatbed glass, blurring the scan past the scanner's depth-of-field).
    The print needs to be peeled, but a 100×100mm single-layer ASA pad
    is too fragile to peel without tearing. Solution: add a multi-layer
    rectangular ring OUTSIDE the fiducial bbox so the whole print peels
    as one piece. Frame is 4 thin extruded bars (top/bottom/left/right)
    forming the ring. Calibration zones / fiducials / scale bar /
    corner-C loops stay STRICTLY single-layer — only the frame is
    multi-layer. Default 2 layers × 3mm wide × 5mm margin from fiducial
    bbox. New CLI flags:
      --zcal-frame              (default on)
      --zcal-frame-layers N     (default 2, gives 0.40mm peelable height)
      --zcal-frame-width mm     (default 3.0)
      --zcal-frame-margin mm    (default 5.0)
    Metadata + JSON sidecar grow a frame block (outer_bbox, inner_bbox,
    layers, z_max_mm) so AI-vision tooling can ignore the frame region
    during zone analysis. With frame on, total plate footprint expands
    by 2*(margin+frame_width) per side (default: 100mm → 111mm). With
    --zcal-frame=0, behavior matches the prior v4 single-layer plate.
  * CLI: add --calibrate-type retraction-tower / vfa-tower. Both are
    headless ports of Plater::calib_retraction / calib_VFA. Same shape
    as the other towers (load DRC resource, cut to range, per-object
    configs, Print::set_calib_params for per-layer modulation).
    New flags: --retraction-tower-{start,end,step} mm,
    --vfa-tower-{start,end,step} mm/s.
  * CLI: unified calibration outputs (cli_emit_calib_outputs) — replaces
    the Z-cal-only metadata injector with one post-slice emitter that
    covers all 10 calibration types. Adds:
      1. Per-block metadata comments in the gcode header for every type
         (was only Z-cal before). Example for temp tower:
           ; tower_block_0 = z_low=0.00, z_high=10.00, temp_c=235
           ; tower_block_1 = z_low=10.00, z_high=20.00, temp_c=230
         For flow-rate: per-block name + modifier + print_flow_ratio + XY.
         For Z-cal: existing zone/fiducial/scale-bar coordinates.
      2. A <basename>.calib.json sidecar next to the gcode with the same
         content in structured form — AI-vision tooling consumes this
         instead of line-by-line parsing the gcode header. Covers all
         10 calibration types.
    Encodes the per-layer modulation formulas the Print engine uses at
    GCode.cpp:4604-4630 so the AI side doesn't have to invert
    start/end/step from observed defect Z.
  * CLI: dead-code removal of cli_inject_zcal_metadata (the Z-only
    injector). Functionality is now in cli_emit_calib_outputs.
    headless ports of Plater::calib_temp / calib_max_vol_speed /
    _calib_pa_tower. Each loads its tower mesh from resources/calib/,
    cuts it to the user's range, applies per-object + filament + print
    configs line-for-line from the GUI, and sets Print::set_calib_params
    so the gcode emitter modulates temperature / volumetric speed /
    pressure advance per layer at generation time.
    New CLI flags (all default to sensible PLA-range values):
      --temp-tower-start C / --temp-tower-end C
        (default 240→195; 5°C step is GUI-hardcoded)
      --vs-tower-start mm3/s / --vs-tower-end mm3/s / --vs-tower-step mm3/s
        (default 5→20 step 0.5; converted to mm/s internally via flow math
        before set_calib_params, matching the GUI's mm3_per_mm conversion)
      --pa-tower-start s / --pa-tower-end s / --pa-tower-step s
        (default 0.0→0.08 step 0.002; suitable for direct-drive)
    Verified on PLA: temp tower emits M104 at every 10mm Z transition
    (8 blocks, S235→S200), vs tower ramps F1500→F2040 in outer-wall moves
    over 22mm height, PA tower emits SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE per layer.
    PRINT_START contract preserved verbatim.
  * CLI: add --calibrate-type z-offset-pattern — scanner-friendly Z-offset
    calibration plate generated procedurally (TriangleMesh primitives, no
    pre-built 3MF resource). Single-layer plate (0.20mm) with:
      - 4 corner fiducial squares for AI-vision auto-alignment / de-skew
      - 10mm scale bar with 11 ticks for DPI cross-verification
      - Zone S (solid concentric pad) — squish-uniformity indicator
      - Zone G (3 wall-pairs at 0.5/0.6/0.8mm gaps) — under-Z closes gaps,
        over-Z leaves all open
      - Zone W (3 single-perimeter freestanding walls) — over-squish detector
      - Zone C (4 corner loops) — cross-bed Z uniformity
    Coordinate ground-truth (bed-space) of every primitive is post-injected
    as metadata comments at the top of the gcode (calibration_type,
    fiducial_positions, scale_bar_origin, zone_*_center, zone_*_size,
    zone_G_gaps_mm, zone_W_wall_count, zone_C_corners) so AI-vision tooling
    can map scan pixels to test zones without inferring from geometry.
    New flags: --zcal-size mm (default 100), --zcal-zone-size mm (default 30),
    --zcal-fiducials, --zcal-scale-bar, --zcal-zone-labels (v1: off; labels
    are documented in metadata comments instead of as stick-font geometry).
    PRINT_START contract preserved verbatim — the standard --slice 0 pipeline
    handles temperatures + extrusion + start gcode template substitution.
    Estimated print time on a 100mm plate: ~1m 30s at 25mm/s first-layer speed.
  * CLI flow-rate calibration: port the brim auto-spacing logic from the
    GUI (Plater.cpp:13076-13115) into cli_apply_flowrate_calib. The first
    cut set per-block brim configs but left blocks at the 3MF's native
    31mm grid — with 30mm blocks that's only 1mm edge gap, so adjacent
    brims merged. Now scales positions outward from the cluster centroid
    by the smallest uniform factor that widens every gap to
    2*brim_width + brim_extra_gap (matches GUI math line-for-line).
  * CLI flow-rate calibration: port the missing per-object setters
    (print_flow_ratio derived from object name, calib_flowrate_topinfill_
    special_order) and the print-config overrides (layer_height,
    initial_layer_print_height, alternate_extra_wall=false,
    reduce_crossing_wall=true, enable_wrapping_detection=false,
    max_volumetric_extrusion_rate_slope=0). Without these every block
    extruded identically — the test was silently a no-op for flow rate
    variation.
  * CLI flow-rate calibration: --flow-blocks N and --flow-range R for
    subsetting the loaded block-pair objects. Both compose (intersection).
  * debian/rules: skip dh_dwz (orca-slicer's debug info trips the
    "Too many DIEs" guard, and bundled libs lack .debug_info).
  * CLI pa-tower: fix SIGSEGV (exit 139) — guard null ConfigOption
    derefs in the calibration apply path.
  * Root cause: cli_apply_pa_tower runs on the merged config before
    Print::apply() fills defaults, so optional keys line_width and
    outer_wall_speed were absent and dereferenced. The assert() guards
    compile out under NDEBUG (RelWithDebInfo), turning misses into null
    derefs. GUI path passes a fully-defaulted full_config() so it never
    hit this.

  [ Packerlschupfer ]
  * Fixes: calib.cpp find_optimal_PA_speed reads
    outer_wall_speed/nozzle_diameter/filament_max_volumetric_speed with
    ConfigDef-default fallbacks; CalibCLI.cpp guards the line_width
    get_abs_value (0 = auto); Config.cpp two-arg get_abs_value throws
    instead of segfaulting; PrintConfig.cpp --calibrate-type help lists
    all supported types.

